text:
maria: quick check — saw prod logs that include the full user prompt and Authorization header in plaintext. i'm seeing things like:

[INFO] 2024-04-10 14:25:12 req=abc123 prompt="Customer SSN: 123-45-6789. Please verify account info." headers={Authorization: Bearer sk-prod-abcdef123456, X-Request-Id: abc123}

Not sure if this is coming from the llm-proxy or our request logging middleware. Is this acceptable or should we redact at ingest? :grimacing:

alex: thanks for flagging — not acceptable. we should never log raw prompt text or auth headers. can you paste the raw log line (sanitized) and point to the service emitting it?

maria: sanitized below. service is `llm-proxy` v2.3.4 behind the API gateway. exact snippet from logs (i replaced token chars):

[INFO] req=abc123 service=llm-proxy prompt="Customer SSN: 123-45-6789. Please verify." headers={Authorization: Bearer sk-prod-xxxxx..., X-Forwarded-For: 10.2.3.4}

alex: looks like llm-proxy's request-logging middleware is capturing full body. quick mitigation: configure it to mask `Authorization` and `prompt` fields and stop logging bodies for POST /v1/generate except for status/latency. e.g. in our Go middleware config: 

LOG_REDACT_FIELDS=authorization,prompt,ssn,email

and a scrubber function that replaces matched patterns with `«REDACTED»`.

jamie: for implementation here's a tiny Node/Express middleware we can repurpose (works for JSON bodies):

function scrubHeadersAndBody(req, res, next) {
  const redactKeys = ['authorization','prompt','ssn','email'];
  if (req.headers) {
    redactKeys.forEach(k => { if (req.headers[k]) req.headers[k] = '«REDACTED»'; });
  }
  if (req.body && typeof req.body === 'object') {
    const scrub = obj => {
      for (const k in obj) {
        if (redactKeys.includes(k.toLowerCase())) obj[k] = '«REDACTED»';
        else if (typeof obj[k] === 'object') scrub(obj[k]);
      }
    };
    scrub(req.body);
  }
  next();
}

jamie: also add regex-based scrub for common PII patterns (SSN, credit card) at logging time; don't rely only on key names since prompts can contain freeform PII.

sam: agree — we should also run this change by privacy for DLP sign-off. recommended steps: 1) redact at ingress 2) add e2e tests that assert no PII in logs 3) add retention rule to purge masked logs after 30 days by default.

maria: understood. i'll open a PR that adds middleware and a unit test searching recent logs for patterns. one question — what about prompt caching/cold-start traces? we sometimes persist request/response pairs to the cache layer for debugging. need guidance on storing sanitized copies only.

alex: never store raw prompt text in caches. if we need to keep examples for debugging, store a hashed or redacted version and tag with `debug=true` behind an access control. also update the console so only SRE+sec can view unredacted debug samples (with explicit audit).

security-bot: automated reminder — please attach a short risk assessment to the PR and include the CVE/privacy impact checklist. See https://redwood.internal/sec/sop/logging-redaction

ci-bot: CI will run redaction smoke tests after PR creation. failing tests will block merge.

maria: thanks all — i'll aim to open the PR by EOD. tagging @alex @jamie for review. any preference on the sanitizer lib? i can use our in-house `redactlib` or `npm scrubber`.

alex: use `redactlib` (we maintain it), but add a small adapter so the middleware can fall back to regex rules if lib is missing. keep config via env and feature-flag under `FF_LOG_REDACT_ENFORCE` before rolling to prod.

sam: add a short runbook entry for on-call in case the scrubber causes latency or drops logs we need for an incident.

maria: got it — PR incoming. I'll include the runbook, DLP note, and tests. :thumbsup:
